{{visualization:1}}

{{algorithm:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the metric obstruction proof</summary>

An antilipschitz map cannot decrease Hausdorff dimension. A subset of an $m$-dimensional normed space has dimension at most $m$. Thus a map from $\mathbb R^n$ would imply $n\le m$; when $m<n$, no such map exists. The same argument rules out any antilipschitz map from an infinite-Hausdorff-dimensional set into finite-dimensional space.

</details>

For background, see the [Hausdorff dimension overview](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hausdorff_dimension) and the [Hilbert space overview](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hilbert_space).

## A topological address inside the Hilbert cube

Distance-to-landmark coordinates provide bounded continuous measurements. Explore a finite version below.

