{{demo:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal why landmarks separate points</summary>

If $x\ne y$, choose a dense landmark $q$ sufficiently close to $x$. The triangle inequality forces the distances from $q$ to $x$ and $y$ to differ. Applying $t\mapsto t/(1+t)$ keeps the coordinate in $[0,1)$ without losing that distinction. Countably many landmarks yield a topological embedding into $[0,1]^{\mathbb N}$.

</details>

Learn more about the [Hilbert cube](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hilbert_cube) and [separable spaces](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Separable_space).

## Approximation is not global compression

Every square-summable sequence can be approximated by finitely supported rational sequences, although no fixed truncation handles the whole space.

{{algorithm:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

