<details><summary>Reveal the finite-cover theorem</summary>

The Hausdorff dimension of a finite union is the maximum of the dimensions of its pieces. Therefore finitely many finite-dimensional pieces cannot cover a set of infinite Hausdorff dimension. Countably many pieces are different: their finite dimensions may grow without bound.

</details>
